sd_photos: the size cap, the 400 misattribution, T17 provenance -- criteria 2026-10-14.1

A. the server says WHY, instead of blaming a migration
  ok       A1. an upstream 400 carrying pg code 23514 answers 400 ROW_REFUSED. THIS IS THE DEFECT: it used to answer 503 NOT_PROVISIONED -- "run the migration" -- for a table that exists and a row that is too big
  ok       A2. CONTROL -- an upstream 404 still answers 503 NOT_PROVISIONED. Without this arm the fix could be "never say NOT_PROVISIONED again", and a genuinely unmigrated table would stop being diagnosable
  ok       A3. a 400 whose body says the relation does not exist is STILL 503 NOT_PROVISIONED -- the original reason for folding 400 in with 404 was real and is kept, not thrown away with the defect
  ok       A4. THE THIRD STATE -- a 400 that can be classified as neither answers 400 UPSTREAM_REJECTED and says the reason could not be classified. It does NOT say "run the migration". Folding could-not-tell into either of the other two answers is how this defect was built
  ok       A5. CONTROL -- a successful write is still 200, so A1-A4 are not passing because everything now fails
  ok       A6. ZERO (404||400) branches answer 503 NOT_PROVISIONED without reading the body: 89 go through notProvisioned() and 9 discriminate for themselves. A branch that decides blind is the defect, so the arm counts them rather than naming a number
  ok       A7. the 5 call sites that already did `const rows = await r.json()` BEFORE the guard pass that body in, so the helper does not read an already-consumed stream. A Response body can be read once, and a helper that reads it again turns one wrong diagnosis into another
  ok       A8. a call site that had ALREADY consumed the body still classifies correctly -- rf_photos' write reads `rows` before its guard and hands it in, and a 23514 there answers ROW_REFUSED. A7 proves the argument is passed; this proves it is READ, which is the half an ablation showed A7 alone could not see

B. the image is downscaled to fit, measured rather than assumed
  ok       B1. the data-URL budget is 44000 characters -- under the 65,536-byte row cap with 21536 bytes left for the rest of the row, which carries aiAnalysis (3-5 model sentences), ai_source, the ids and the JSON quoting. The budget is NOT the cap and that difference is the point
  ok       B2. the ladder is WALKED and the result MEASURED: 2 rung(s) tried, the winner is 1280px/q0.72 at 29491 bytes, under the 44000 budget. The first rung that actually fits wins -- the length is checked, not predicted from the dimensions
  ok       B3. when NO rung fits the budget the result is null -- not the original, and not a silent pass-through. A downscaler that returns the original on failure is how an oversized row reaches the server looking like a downscaled one
  ok       B4. handlePhotoFiles stores the DOWNSCALED data URL when the downscale succeeded, and falls back to the original only when it did not -- so the row that is written is the row that was measured
  ok       B5. a FAILED downscale tells the user the photo is on this device only. The local copy is kept -- refusing it would lose the photograph to protect a backup -- but the silence is what ends

C. T17 -- provenance on the QC note
  ok       C1. sd_photos resolves in EXACTLY ONE of HIGH_RISK_AI / NOT_HIGH_RISK_AI (high=false, not-high=true). Unclassified fails: the b18 spec requires the decision to exist, not to go a particular way
  ok       C2. ...and the not-high-risk entry says WHY IN TERMS OF THE APP rather than the content: sd_photos is not in SD_SESSION_GATED, so an approval gate would refuse every analysed photo on an app with no sign-in requirement. The entry names the order a promotion has to happen in
  ok       C3. all four photo-QC siblings are in AI_SCANNED_TEXT, driven by ONE loop: rf_photos, bld_photo_analyses, grd_progress_photos, sd_photos. sd_photos was the one that was missed, and a per-resource arm would have been written for the three that were already there
  ok       C4. sd_photos' redacted field is `aiAnalysis` and NOT `data` -- the model prose is redacted and the base64 photograph is not, because redacting an image payload corrupts the image
  ok       C5. photoAnalyze stamps ai_source = claude:photo-qc on the row. Before this batch `grep -c "ai_source" stonedesk.html` was 0 for the photo path: aiAnalysis was d.content[0].text verbatim with no source, no approver and no model id
  ok       C6. and NOTHING assigns ai_approved_by on this path. The write happens the moment the model answers, so there is no approver; aiFooter() reads "AI-assisted - not reviewed", which is the true sentence
  ok       C8. the render carries a label that is NOT the spinner string. "Analyzing with Claude..." is overwritten by the answer the instant it arrives, so a page whose only AI wording is the spinner has no AI wording at all -- the b18 spec names exactly that
  ok       C9. ...and it is rendered on BOTH success paths -- the cached analysis and the fresh one (3 call sites). A label on one of two paths is a label that is absent half the time

D. the failure reaches a human
  ok       D1. sdDataFailed, RUN against a stub, reports to a HUMAN: "\"sd_photos\" was NOT backed up — the server refused the record as too large. It is saved on". Its whole body was one console.warn, while the platform's own comment in sdSyncCollection already said the argument -- "silence would make a backup that is not happening look like one that is"
  ok       D1b. ...and the row-too-large case is NAMED, because it is the one a user can act on. The server now answers 400 ROW_REFUSED for a CHECK violation instead of 503 "tables are not set up yet", so the reason that reaches this function is a real one
  ok       D2. ...and the console line is KEPT. The toast is for the user, the warn is for whoever reads a console later, and removing one to add the other would trade one audience for another
  ok       D3. every branch of it is guarded and showToast is reached through a typeof test, so this failure path cannot itself fail -- the rule sdData's own catch records paying for twice
  ok       D4. a burst is summarised rather than toasted per record. One toast per failure on a sync of 200 photos is its own kind of silence, because a user dismisses the first three and stops reading

